# Code owners.
#
# The point is not that these files are hard to change — it is that they are hard to
# change *without a second person looking*. Each path below is one where a quiet edit
# changes what the platform is allowed to answer or what it costs to run.

# The default owner for anything not named below.
* @your-org/data-platform

# Data contracts are the platform's constitution: a change here changes what an agent
# may do, and it must be reviewed by whoever owns the product.
/contracts/ @your-org/data-governance @your-org/data-platform

# The semantic layer decides which definition of "revenue" wins. Metric fragments
# become SQL that goes to a regulator.
/semantic/ @your-org/data-governance @your-org/analytics

# Infrastructure: a change here can widen an IAM policy or delete a lake. The
# permissions boundary limits the damage architecturally; this limits it socially.
/infra/ @your-org/platform-security @your-org/data-platform

# The bootstrap stack creates the deploy roles and the boundary itself. Changes
# require a security review, because a mistake here is not contained by anything.
/infra/bootstrap/ @your-org/platform-security

# Agent prompts and tools: these decide refusals and tool access, and they are code.
/src/banking_data_agents/agents/ @your-org/ai-governance @your-org/data-platform
/src/banking_data_agents/tools/ @your-org/ai-governance

# The evaluation harness is the thing that proves everything above still works, so it
# cannot be weakened in the same pull request that weakens an agent.
/src/banking_data_agents/evals/ @your-org/ai-governance @your-org/data-platform

# CI/CD: whoever can change a workflow can deploy.
/.github/workflows/ @your-org/platform-security @your-org/data-platform

# The container image is the artifact that ships, including its user and its
# filesystem permissions.
/Dockerfile @your-org/platform-security
